          "id": "de7a886b59bddba3cb93c83a8d41e997f2bb23f9",
          "message": "{Auth} Leverage parameter set to simplify client side validation (#1126)\n\n**Related command**\n<!-- If applicable, provide the related command, for example: az vm\ncreate, az login, or Az.Compute. -->\naz login / Connect-AzAccount\n\n**Description**\n<!-- Why this PR? What changed? What is the user or maintainer impact?\n-->\nThis pull request introduces a new utility for parameter set validation\nand resolution in the Accounts extension, enabling more robust and\nmaintainable handling of command-line parameters. Plus some small\nrefactoring.\n\nThe concept of this parameter sets is deliberately internal to the\nAccounts extension for now until we have time to tackle the problem at a\nbigger scale.\n\nBesides, the solution doesn't utilize the parameter attributes or a\nparameter group's attributes that are already in the implementation. I\nprefer to defer that when we want to elevate the solution to every\nextension.\n\n### Design\n\nA **parameter set** is a named combination of parameters that must be\nused together (like PowerShell's `ParameterSetName`). The model is\ndeliberately simple with one assumption: a parameter is *supplied* when\nits value is non-null, a non-empty string, or a `true` switch.\nEverything lives in `Extensions/Accounts/ParameterSets` (internal) and\nis currently used only by `LoginProcessor`.\n\n```csharp\nprotected override void DefineParameterSets()\n{\n    Resolver.DefineSet(\"DeviceCode\")\n        .WithMandatory(c => ((LoginProcessor)c).UseDeviceCode)    // all mandatory must be supplied\n        .WithMandatory(c => ((LoginProcessor)c).SubscriptionId)\n        .WithOptional(c => ((LoginProcessor)c).TenantId)          // may be supplied\n        .WithOptional(c => ((LoginProcessor)c).OutProfile);\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nA set *applies* when every mandatory parameter is supplied **and**\nnothing outside its mandatory + optional parameters is supplied.\n\n**Flow**\n\n1. `LoginProcessorBase.Init` creates the resolver, calls\n`DefineParameterSets()` and then `Validate()`, so a bad definition fails\nat startup, not at some user's invocation.\n2. `ProcessAsync` calls `ResolveParameterSet()`, which resolves exactly\none set from the supplied arguments. Failure is rethrown as\n`InvalidInput` with parameter names formatted for the surface\n(`--subscription-id` / `-SubscriptionId`).\n3. `ValidateArguments(parameterSet)` runs only the checks that sets\ncannot express (value formats, \"at most one MI selector\", etc.).\n\nLogin's sets: `ManagedIdentity`, `DeviceCode`, `FederatedIdentity`,\n`ProfileReauth`, `Browser`.\n\n#### What the framework handles for you\n\n* **Mutual exclusivity is proven** `Validate()` guarantees the\ndefinition of parameter sets are mutually exclusive.\n* **Resolution.** Maps the supplied arguments to the single matching\nset.\n* **Simplified parameter validation** No hand-written `if (a && b)\nthrow` checks. Unresolvable input yields an exception.\n\n### Refactorings\n\n* Changed `LoginProcessor` properties (`SubscriptionId`, `TenantId`,\n`UseDeviceCode`, and `OutProfile`) to be nullable, allowing for more\naccurate detection of user-supplied versus default values and aligning\nwith the new parameter set logic.\n* Updated the `ISubscriptionService.ResolveSubscriptionTenantAsync`\ninterface and its implementation to accept a nullable `subscriptionId`,\naligning with the changes to `LoginProcessor` and improving\nnull-handling throughout the authentication flow.\n[[1]](diffhunk://#diff-e4e6056c6f88d69353f790a79256ecf0cb01b1176af1d36a6f03a161030e01e9L24-R24)\n[[2]](diffhunk://#diff-976d167a66bc3ae830787509bd0733b99acd7acf7595f6d19a31b36662963943L61-R61)\n\n\n**Testing Guide**\n<!-- List validation commands or manual test steps. -->\n- There is no syntax or behavioral changes. All unit tests and scenario\ntests pass.\n\n**History Notes**\n<!--\nRelease notes are generated from PR metadata during stable release\npreparation.\n\nUse [Component Name] for customer-facing changes that should appear in\nrelease notes.\nUse {Component Name} for internal, test-only, docs-only, or\nrelease-process changes that should not appear in release notes.\n\nPR titles must not end with trailing punctuation or trailing spaces.\n\nIf one PR needs multiple release-note entries, or if the PR title is\nonly a summary, add notes below in this format:\n\n[Component Name] `az command`: Add customer-facing behavior\n[Component Name] BREAKING CHANGE: `az command`: Remove deprecated\nbehavior\n\nHistory note lines must not end with trailing punctuation or trailing\nspaces.\n\nCommon components: CLI, PowerShell, Agent, Auth, Packaging, DevOps,\nDocs.\nDelete this section only when no release note is needed.\n-->\n\n---\n\n- [x] The PR title starts with `[Component Name]` for customer-facing\nchanges or `{Component Name}` for non-customer-facing changes, and does\nnot end with trailing punctuation or spaces.\n- [ ] Customer-facing changes include a useful release note in the PR\ntitle or the **History Notes** section.\n- [x] I added or updated tests for behavior changes.\n- [x] I updated documentation when behavior, setup, support, or security\nguidance changed.",
